// rakun-bpp/server — the request as a record, and the server-component
// convention.
//
// Promoted from `server.d.bp` (front 28 of 1.0.10-beta). The declaration file
// listed three blockers and all three are answered rather than carried:
//
//   - "the async SSR data layer … is gated on the effect-await surface" is
//     CLOSED. `fn … -> Async<T>` with a statement-level `await` compiles and
//     RUNS on both rows, `test` blocks included — measured here, not assumed,
//     and every `Async` assertion in `test/server_test.bp` executes on the
//     erlang row.
//   - "`request()` is host-bound" stays true, and the host half ships WITH the
//     module, cell for cell on both rows, exactly as front 26's router does.
//   - "`Http` is a phantom `@Context` base (no members)" is DROPPED, along with
//     the `Request` behavior. `request()` is a hook anchored at `ElementBase`
//     (decisions 104/128 of 1.0.10-beta), so one base serves the whole render
//     tree; a second, memberless base would be a base nothing implements. `AGENTS.md` § *The `Http` base, and why it is gone*
//     carries the reasoning.
//
// ── Two things about this file that are NOT the front's README ──────────────
//
// 1. The six cells carry BOTH targets and name `jhonstart_server`, not
//    `rakun_request_context`.
//
//    The README binds them to front 62's erlang module. That is not writable,
//    and the reason is a measurement in each direction:
//
//      - An erlang-only cell reds the **commonJS compile** at its call site the
//        moment `request()` calls it — `` `__jhReqMethod` has no
//        `#[compiler.external(.<Target>(…))]` for the node backend `` — and this module is
//        compiled on both rows of the core member, so the whole member (51
//        landed assertions) stops compiling. Front 26 measured the same thing
//        for the router's five reads and carries the same note.
//      - `rakun_request_context` has no BEAM existence to bind to. rakun's
//        member is `targets: ["commonJS"]`, so on the erlang row — this front's
//        assigned target — every cell would answer `{error,undef}` at runtime
//        and no assertion that reads the request could RUN. Measured 2026-09-21
//        against compiler `2e6bb4ac`.
//
//    So the seam is HERE and it is `pub`, which is the conclusion front 26
//    already reached for the route snapshot and wrote down: a server that
//    reached into another library's process dictionary keys would be coupled to
//    them forever. `enterRequest(req)` is the one writer and `leaveRequest()`
//    its pair; front 30's render calls both, once per render, with the
//    `RequestData` rakun-ssr handed it (decision 114 item 8).
//
// 2. The four pair-shaped values are stored `encoding.formStringify`-encoded
//    and read back with `encoding.formParse` — std's percent-aware codec, the
//    one rakun uses on the server (decision 116 rule 4). A cookie or query
//    value carrying `%20` reads as a space here exactly as it does in rakun.

// ── `Async` is EAGER on erlang, and that changes how a page is written ───────
//
// `libs/std/src/http.bp:16-18` states it: Erlang is eager, a `Async<T>`
// resolves to `T`, so the caller's `await` is identity on that
// backend (botopink decision 120). Two `Async` loaders do NOT run in parallel
// because they are Tasks — they run in the order the body reaches them, and the
// page costs the SUM of its loaders.
//
// So a page with INDEPENDENT loaders does not await them one after another and
// hope. Parallel loading on the BEAM is a spawned process per unit of work,
// gathered by index, it takes **unstarted tasks** (`Array<fn() -> Async<T>>`,
// i.e. `[{ -> loadPost(slug) }, { -> loadSidebar() }]`) rather than Tasks that
// have already run, and it is **front 02's**. This file ships no `awaitAll`,
// no `race` and no `allSettled`, and `conformance/docs.md` § *Server components* says who
// owns them.
//
// ── Where an `await` may stand ──────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// At statement level in a `Async` / `Component` body, and nowhere else. Never as the
// last statement of a lambda: `§2.38` makes a lambda's last statement an
// implicit-return expression and no file in this tree awaits inside one. A
// component that needs N rows awaits ONE loader returning `Array<T>` and maps
// synchronously afterwards — not N awaits inside a `map`.
//
// ── Escaping is not this file's ─────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// `renderToString` writes `e.value` and every attribute value straight into the
// output (`element.bp:56`, `:63-65`) and neither escapes. A server component
// renders attacker-influenced text, so every such value goes through std's
// `escape.html` / `escape.attribute` — front 30's `renderNode` calls them for
// every text node and attribute, and a component that builds a string for
// markup by hand calls them at the point the value enters the tree. This file
// hand-rolls no escaping and re-exports none.

import {element.Element, element.ElementBase, element.renderToString};
import {router.pairValue, router.markDynamic};
import {encoding, compiler} from "std";

// ── step 1: the request as a record ─────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Six fields, every plural one an `Array<#(string, string)>` — the shape front
// 26's snapshot uses, the shape `encoding.formParse` produces and the shape
// `Element.attrs` takes, so a value read off the request is handed straight to
// an attribute with no conversion. No `Dict`: naming `dict.Dict<string, string>`
// as a type across a module boundary is unexercised anywhere in this tree, and
// the pair list is what actually crosses the wire.
//
// There is no `body` field, and its absence is a decision rather than an
// omission: a render never reads one. Form bodies are front 24's and
// route-handler bodies are front 25's.
//
// Field names and method names are kept DISJOINT, the same rule `RouterState`
// follows — `params` the field, `param(name)` the method, `query` the field,
// `queryParam(name)` the method — so a field read never shadows a method.
pub type RequestData(
    method: string,
    path: string,
    params: Array<#(string, string)>,
    query: Array<#(string, string)>,
    headers: Array<#(string, string)>,
    cookies: Array<#(string, string)>,
) {
    // Every accessor answers a plain `string`, `""` when absent, and never
    // raises. That is the ecosystem's decided shape and not laziness: rakun's
    // `Request` does exactly this (`repository/rakun/src/http.bp:30-34`). An
    // optional would force `.unwrapOr` at every call site in every page, and
    // `?T` handling is where the old drafts of this front went wrong.
    pub fn param(self: Self, name: string) -> string {
        return pairValue(self.params, name);
    }

    pub fn queryParam(self: Self, name: string) -> string {
        return pairValue(self.query, name);
    }

    pub fn header(self: Self, name: string) -> string {
        return pairValue(self.headers, name);
    }

    pub fn cookie(self: Self, name: string) -> string {
        return pairValue(self.cookies, name);
    }
}

// ── step 2: the host cells and the request builder ──────────────────────────
//
// Six reads and one write, dual-target for the reason at the top of this file.
// `src/sidecars/jhonstart_server.erl` and `src/server_runtime.mjs` are the two
// halves, cell for cell, so `test/server_test.bp` is ONE set of assertions run
// on both rows.
//
// The four pair-shaped values travel querystring-encoded (`k=v&k=v`), the same
// encoding front 26's `m`/`q` use and the same encoding front 23's payload
// carries. No JSON, no record serialization, nothing that has to agree between
// an Erlang term and a JS object.

#[compiler.external(.JavaScript("require('./server_runtime.mjs').method()"), .Erlang("jhonstart_server:method()"))]
declare fn __jhReqMethod() -> string;

#[compiler.external(.JavaScript("require('./server_runtime.mjs').path()"), .Erlang("jhonstart_server:path()"))]
declare fn __jhReqPath() -> string;

#[compiler.external(.JavaScript("require('./server_runtime.mjs').params()"), .Erlang("jhonstart_server:params()"))]
declare fn __jhReqParams() -> string;

#[compiler.external(.JavaScript("require('./server_runtime.mjs').query()"), .Erlang("jhonstart_server:query()"))]
declare fn __jhReqQuery() -> string;

#[compiler.external(.JavaScript("require('./server_runtime.mjs').headers()"), .Erlang("jhonstart_server:headers()"))]
declare fn __jhReqHeaders() -> string;

#[compiler.external(.JavaScript("require('./server_runtime.mjs').cookies()"), .Erlang("jhonstart_server:cookies()"))]
declare fn __jhReqCookies() -> string;

// The writer pair, and the seam front 30's render calls once per render with
// the `RequestData` rakun-ssr handed it (decision 114 item 8). Outside `server.bp`
// and front 30's `render.bp` nothing in `src/` calls either.
//
// Six values in TOGETHER, never one at a time, for the reason front 26's `fill`
// gives about the route: a half-updated request is a component reading the
// previous reader's cookie against this reader's path.
//
// Note that this is NOT front 26's `fill` under another name and the two stores
// stay separate on purpose. A route snapshot is re-filled DURING a render — its
// `selected` is the layout depth and changes per layout — while a request is
// entered once and is constant for the whole render. Folding them together
// would make "the request" mutate mid-page.
//
// The `-> i32` is the ecosystem's shape for a host cell whose value is not used
// (`router.bp`'s `__jhRouteFill`, `rakun/src/runtime.bp`'s `rkScan`). It is not
// a status code and callers ignore it.
#[compiler.external(
    .JavaScript("require('./server_runtime.mjs').enter($0, $1, $2, $3, $4, $5)"),
    .Erlang("jhonstart_server:enter($0, $1, $2, $3, $4, $5)"),
)]
declare fn __jhReqEnter(
    method: string,
    path: string,
    params: string,
    query: string,
    headers: string,
    cookies: string,
) -> i32;

#[compiler.external(.JavaScript("require('./server_runtime.mjs').leave()"), .Erlang("jhonstart_server:leave()"))]
declare fn __jhReqLeave() -> i32;

// `1` between an `enterRequest` and its `leaveRequest`, `0` otherwise.
#[compiler.external(.JavaScript("require('./server_runtime.mjs').entered()"), .Erlang("jhonstart_server:entered()"))]
declare fn __jhReqEntered() -> i32;

// Enter the request for the render that follows: the four pair lists go in
// `encoding.formStringify`-encoded, the form the cells store.
pub fn enterRequest(req: RequestData) -> i32 {
    return __jhReqEnter(
        req.method,
        req.path,
        encoding.formStringify(req.params),
        encoding.formStringify(req.query),
        encoding.formStringify(req.headers),
        encoding.formStringify(req.cookies),
    );
}

// Leave it: the store is emptied, and a `request()` / `cookies()` /
// `headers()` read afterwards raises — a component that reads the request
// outside a render is a component reading the previous reader's cookie.
pub fn leaveRequest() -> i32 {
    return __jhReqLeave();
}

fn requireEntered(reader: string) -> i32 {
    assert __jhReqEntered() == 1, "rakun-bpp: "
        + reader
        + " read outside a render — the request is entered by the render (enterRequest) and left when it ends";
    return 1;
}

// The request the render reads. No `?T` unwrap that can fail:
// `encoding.formParse` answers `[]` for an empty string and every cell answers
// a total value. Read OUTSIDE a render — before `enterRequest` or after
// `leaveRequest` — it raises: there is no request to read, and an empty one
// would be a silent wrong answer. A component that must render with no host
// takes a `RequestData` as a parameter instead.
//
// A hook (decisions 104/128 of 1.0.10-beta): `val r = use request()` in a
// server component — `fn … -> Component<Element>`, which may
// also `await` because `Component` extends `Async`. Called without `use` it
// answers the same `Component` (a Promise on commonJS), so a `Async` body or a
// test `await`s it. It cannot fail, so its `T` is not a `Result`.
//
// Reading the request marks the render DYNAMIC (router.bp's `markDynamic`):
// its output depends on this reader's headers, cookies or query, so a cache
// may not keep it as static.
pub component fn request() -> Component<RequestData> {
    requireEntered("request()");
    markDynamic();
    return RequestData(
        method: __jhReqMethod(),
        path: __jhReqPath(),
        params: encoding.formParse(__jhReqParams()),
        query: encoding.formParse(__jhReqQuery()),
        headers: encoding.formParse(__jhReqHeaders()),
        cookies: encoding.formParse(__jhReqCookies()),
    );
}

// The only two re-exported shortcuts, because they are the two Next.js names a
// page reaches for without wanting the whole request. `after`, `connection`,
// `draftMode` and per-request memoization are front 62's and are called from
// there directly — re-declaring them here would be a second place for request
// scope to be defined.
pub fn cookies() -> Array<#(string, string)> {
    requireEntered("cookies()");
    return encoding.formParse(__jhReqCookies());
}

pub fn headers() -> Array<#(string, string)> {
    requireEntered("headers()");
    return encoding.formParse(__jhReqHeaders());
}

// ── step 3: the server-component convention ─────────────────────────────────
//
// A server component is a `pub fn` taking its route params and returning
// `Async<Element>`. There is no decorator for it and there will not be one: the
// marker is the return type itself (botopink decision 118 — the return is the
// annotation), and the language ENFORCES it: a body that `await`s under a
// return that is not a `Async` (or above) is `effect-await-without-task`, and a
// second marker would be a second thing to get wrong. A server component that
// also activates a hook is `fn … -> Component<Element>` (decision
// 128: `Async` grants no `use`, and `Component` extends `Async`, so that one
// return grants `use` and `await` both); `renderComponent` renders its thunk.
//
// Neither return is a `Result`, so a component cannot propagate a failed load
// with `try` (decision 121): it handles the error in its own body — `try … catch`,
// `case`, or an error screen. A loader that can fail returns
// `Async<Result<T, E>>` and the component writes `try await load(…) catch …`.
//
// The parameter is `fn() -> Async<Element>` — an UNSTARTED thunk, not an
// already-running `Async<Element>`. That is the same shape front 02's
// spawn-and-gather takes, so a page that grows a second loader moves from
// `renderServerComponent({ -> Page(params) })` to front 02 without changing what
// it hands anybody.
//
// **Call it with a lambda, never with a bare function name.** `{ -> Page(ps) }`
// is green on both rows; `renderServerComponent(Page)` compiles on commonJS and
// fails `variable 'Page' is unbound` on erlang — a compiler defect measured
// against `2e6bb4ac` and reported, not worked around. It is the same rule
// rakun's front 23 records for the fields of its `ElementView`.
pub async fn renderServerComponent(
    component: fn() -> Async<Element>,
) -> Async<string> {
    val tree = await component();
    return renderToString(tree);
}

// A server component that activates a hook is `fn … ->
// Component<Element>` (decision 128 of botopink 1.0.10-beta: only a
// `Component` return grants `use`, and `Component` extends `Async`, so the same
// body may `await` too). Its thunk is rendered here, as `renderServerComponent`
// renders a `Async` one.
pub async fn renderComponent(
    component: fn() -> Component<Element>,
) -> Async<string> {
    val tree = await component();
    return renderToString(tree);
}
